sweep:latin-late-antique.corpus_overview:
I checked about 48 Latin secular texts and corpora dated c. 200–700. Sources: the ancientrome.ru catalogue, the vostlit.info А–Я indexes, the simposium.ru source list, Russian and English Wikipedia bibliographies, Open Library and archive.org. The WebSearch quota ran out early, so translations published in journals (cyberleninka, elibrary) were not searched exhaustively.

Russian coverage of historiography is much denser than expected. About 22 items have full Russian translations:
- almost all MGH Chronica minora on vostlit (Hydatius, Prosper, Marcellinus Comes, Victor of Tunnuna, John of Biclaro, Marius of Avenches, the Gallic Chronicles of 452/511, Cassiodorus' Chronica);
- Anonymus Valesianus, De rebus bellicis, Jordanes' Romana, Ampelius;
- Claudian (2008), Ausonius, Corippus (2017; 2023), Dracontius' mythological poems (2001);
- Q. Serenus (2000), Gargilius (1980), Vegetius' Epitoma, the Ravenna Cosmography, Avienus' Ora maritima.

About 9 are only partial in Russian: Panegyrici Latini (4 speeches online), Symmachus, Sidonius, Variae I–II, Servius (Aen. 1), Solinus, Codex Theodosianus selections, Gromatici excerpts, Avienus' Descriptio.

About 17 have no Russian translation found: Julius Valerius, Itinerarium Alexandri, the Metz Epitome, Ennodius, Marcellus, Pelagonius, Mulomedicina Chironis, Vegetius' Mulomedicina, Cassius Felix, Theodorus Priscianus, Firmicus' Mathesis, Notitia Dignitatum, Expositio, Avienus' Aratea, Fragmenta Vaticana, Liber Pontificalis and the Chronograph of 354.

About 9 of these also have no English translation, but most exist in French, German or Italian. Texts with no translation in any modern language are rare. The best gaps are epistolography, the Latin Alexander corpus and technical medicine.

sweep:ane-egypt.corpus_overview:
The corpus is enormous: hundreds of thousands of cuneiform tablets and tens of thousands of Demotic papyri. Translation coverage depends heavily on genre. Russian translations (Дьяконов, Афанасьева, Емельянов, Козырева, Кацнельсон, Лившиц) cover epic, myth, wisdom, laws and royal inscriptions. Technical and scholarly genres are almost untouched in Russian. I found no Russian translation of:
- any omen series (Enūma Anu Enlil: 68–70 tablets, 6,500–7,000 omens, less than half even in English);
- the 882 cuneiform commentaries in the CCP catalogue;
- the Astronomical Diaries or the Hellenistic chronicles;
- Sakikkû (40 tablets, 3,000+ entries);
- the Persepolis Fortification archive (over 15,000 Elamite records, about 5% published);
- the Murašû archive (650–879 tablets).

English coverage is much better (ETCSL, about 400 Sumerian compositions; SAA; Sachs–Hunger; Hallock). The gaps are these:
- Enūma Anu Enlil 1–6 exists only in Italian.
- Of the Edfu inscriptions, only Edfou VI–VIII exist in German.
- Many Ptolemaic temple texts have no translation in any language.

Egypt: Russian Wikipedia says there is no Russian translation of the Coffin Texts. Demotic literature is mostly untranslated in Russian, but a 1958 popular anthology (Кацнельсон) does contain Setne and two Petubastis tales. So every "untranslated" claim has to be checked.

Free sources exist: the CCP editions (CC BY-SA 4.0, whose ShareAlike clause conflicts with an NC licence) and public-domain editions (Thureau-Dangin 1921; Spiegelberg 1914/1917; Griffith–Thompson 1904; Clay 1898–1912). LLMs handle formulaic Akkadian and Aramaic from transliteration best, and Demotic and Ptolemaic hieroglyphs worst.

Caveat: web search was heavily rate-limited in this session. "RU none" means none found in ru.wikipedia, CyberLeninka and archive.org checks.
